    "additionalCriteria": "Read more about Admission criteria for applicants with recent secondary education.",
    "fees": "Visit the ACU website for general information about tuition fees .",
    "summary": "The Bachelor of Early Childhood Education (Birth to Five Years) is designed for students dedicated to a career teaching in prior to school settings. Early childhood teachers help lay critical learning and development foundations for young children, teaching them the building blocks of numeracy, literacy, music, art and literature. Graduates of this course will have a strong grounding in early childhood curriculum and pedagogy and will have the confidence and practical experience to teach across all year levels from birth to five years.",
    "careers": "Early childhood teacher, kindergarten teacher, policy developer.",
    "practicalExperience": "Students must complete professional experience units in relevant prior-to-school settings in line with the requirements of national authorities. During this time they are required to be available full-time. All enrolled students must have the appropriate approvals before they may enter a classroom: Working with Vulnerable People (ACT) or Working with Children Check (NSW). International students must bring police check documentation from their own countries. All students who have lived in another country for 12 months or more must provide a police check from that country for that period. Supervised professional experience is at the heart of all our education courses and the faculty will find appropriate placements to suit the needs of your course. During your required 80 days of professional experience, you’ll be placed in relevant prior-to-school setting at different stages of your course to ensure your practical experience evolves alongside your theoretical knowledge base. 80 days of professional placements will be arranged for the scheduled placement period in the professional experience calendar.",
    "uacUrl": "https://uac.edu.au/course-search/search/undergraduate/course/101120",
