    "fees": "Refer to UTS current fee information .",
    "summary": "The Bachelor of Communication in Social and Political Sciences at UTS is designed for those passionate about influencing key areas of public policy such as climate change, human rights, inequality, or social justice for marginalised groups. In this course, you’ll explore current social issues, understand their history, and analyse how social change occurs. You’ll work with experienced practitioners and scholars to develop your skills in research, analysis, critical thinking, effective communication and advocacy, and bring key policy issues to life with your interpersonal and public communication skills. This course provides you with a theoretical framework rooted in historical and philosophical perspectives on how societies function, bolstering this with applied learning in qualitative and quantitative research methods. You’ll gain practical skills in interviewing, observation, surveying, archival research, data analysis and policy evaluation. You’ll also learn how to tackle key social issues and make a real difference through research and communication, contributing to new and ongoing debates. Stand out from the crowd and graduate with a multifaceted skill set to bring to a diverse range of government, not-for-profit and private sector organisations striving for social change. To increase your career flexibility, you can add a second stream from five of the other streams offered in the Bachelor of Communication, and build further skills and portfolios that equip you for a range of work options in media and communication. These courses give you practical and transferrable skills for the expanding media industries. Students who wish to study overseas during their course can undertake a UTS exchange or study abroad placement that counts towards their degree, any time after their first year. Communication courses are offered over 3 years full-time or 6 years part-time. The UTS School of Communication offers postgraduate qualifications in various professional communication disciplines. For further information, visit the UTS School of Communication website .",
    "careers": "Advocacy in a variety of field such as environmental, Indigenous affairs, human rights, overseas development organisations and trade unions; policy research, analysis and program management in government and private organisations; management in social services and welfare, including women's, migrant and Indigenous programs; research in think tanks and academia.",
    "practicalExperience": "All subjects provide students with practical research skills that are applied in real-world settings. Several subjects include significant projects, with the final subject allowing students to work on topical issues and be placed with a community, corporate or government organisation, or within NSW Parliament. Students can also elect to undertake a professional internship in industry.",
